# {{ project_name }}

This folder is the starting point for {{ project_name }}. It holds the project's data instructions, code,
research decisions, and results. Add the research question here when the project takes shape.

A **quick check** is a one-minute test that the project's software runs on your computer. It uses invented
data, so you need no data downloads or accounts. In a new project it is a working template example: **PASS**
means the example ran and your basic setup works. It does not show that this project's real data or research
results are correct. Replace `quick_check.py` before using PASS as evidence about this project.

## Quick start

The grey boxes below are commands to type into a terminal. Use **PowerShell** on Windows or **Terminal** on
Mac. First move into the project folder; replace the example path with its real location on your computer.

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
cd C:\path\to\{{ project_slug }}
```

Mac (Terminal):

```sh
cd /path/to/{{ project_slug }}
```

`uv` sets up Python and the packages this project needs. If `uv.lock` is missing, type `uv lock` once to
create the list of exact package versions. Then type these commands:

```text
uv sync --locked
uv run reprokit quick-check
```

`uv sync --locked` installs exactly the versions in `uv.lock`; `uv run` uses that setup to run the quick check.
If your terminal says `uv` is not found, type `python -m pip install uv` to install it, then replace `uv` in
the commands above with `python -m uv`. See the [uv installation guide](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/) if Python is also missing.

## After the quick check

Type `uv run reprokit validate` to check the project's settings and source descriptions. To run real data,
use the build command listed for a deliverable in `project.yaml` after the project's pipeline is added.
This template has no real-data build yet. `protocol.md` explains the research decisions; `project.yaml`
lists the planned outputs and build commands; the [verification note](notes/verification.md) records what
someone actually checked.

After a real run, read [RUN_SUMMARY.md](outputs/RUN_SUMMARY.md) for the measured results. That page appears
when the real run is built; the template's quick check does not create it.

## If the quick check fails

`quick_check.py` is the file in this project folder that runs the invented-data test. The template version
is a placeholder; edit that file when this project gets a real pipeline. If the quick check fails, read its
message, run `uv sync --locked` if it asks you to restore packages, and then type
`uv run reprokit quick-check` again.

## Words used in the run summary

| Word | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Window | The dates covered by the study. |
| Row count | The number of records in the final table. |
| Time | How long a step took when it last ran. |
| Memory | How much working space in the computer the step used at its peak. |
| Checksum | A fingerprint of a file; matching checksums mean the two file copies are identical. |

## Data

The `.gitattributes` file keeps data files and test examples unchanged when Git copies them to another computer.

Every data source has a descriptor in `data/descriptors/` saying what it is, how it was queried, its terms of
use, and what to look for if you need the same kind of data for another place. Raw data is never stored in this
repository.

## Accounts and keys

`.env` is a local file in this project folder for account names and keys needed by data providers. Create it
from the example file: on Windows (PowerShell), run `copy .env.example .env`; on Mac (Terminal), run
`cp .env.example .env`. Add one `NAME=value` line per credential, using the exact variable name expected by
the provider's fetch code (for example, `PROVIDER_API_KEY=your-key`). The names in `.env.example` are examples;
replace them with real names when adding a fetch step. In that fetch step, import
`load_project_env` from `reprokit.env` and call `load_project_env(project_root)` before reading keys. Values already set in your
environment take precedence. `.env` is git-ignored and must never be committed.

